### Why it happens  

`map.addLayer()` without a second argument appends the new layer **after every existing layer**.  
Since MapLibre draws layers from bottom-to-top, the new layer ends up **covering the street-name
symbol layers** that are normally on top of the basemap.

### Fix – insert the layer *before* the first symbol layer  

1. **Find the first symbol layer in the current style** (the one that draws the labels).  
2. Add (or move) your data layer **before** that layer.

```js
// 1️⃣  Get the list of layers that are already in the style
const styleLayers = map.getStyle().layers;

// 2️⃣  Find the first layer whose type is "symbol"
//     (these are the label / icon layers)
const firstSymbol = styleLayers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');

// 3️⃣  Use its id as the insertion point
if (firstSymbol) {
  map.addLayer(
    {
      id: 'my-geojson',
      type: 'fill',               // or line, circle, etc.
      source: 'my-geojson-src',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#ff6600',
        'fill-opacity': 0.4
      }
    },
    firstSymbol.id   // ← insert *before* the first label layer
  );
} else {
  // No symbol layer found – just add it (it will be on top)
  map.addLayer({ /* …layer definition… */ });
}
```

#### If the layer is already added

```js
// Move an existing layer to sit below the labels
if (firstSymbol) {
  map.moveLayer('my-geojson', firstSymbol.id);
}
```

### How it works

- **`addLayer(layer, beforeId)`** – the optional `beforeId` tells MapLibre to place the new
  layer *just before* the layer whose id you pass.  
- **`moveLayer(layerId, beforeId)`** – re-orders an existing layer in the same way.

Because the label layers are of type `"symbol"` and usually the *last* layers in a style,
inserting before the first `"symbol"` guarantees that all your data stays **under** the
street names, POI labels, and any other icons.

### Quick-check checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| ✅ | Identify the first `"symbol"` layer (`map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')`). |
| ✅ | Add your layer with `map.addLayer(yourLayer, firstSymbol.id)`. |
| ✅ | If the layer already exists, call `map.moveLayer('your-layer-id', firstSymbol.id)`. |
| ✅ | Verify in the browser’s *Layers* panel (or `console.log(map.getStyle().layers)`) that your layer now appears **below** the label layers. |

That’s all you need – the map will render your GeoJSON data while the street names and other
labels stay visible on top.

You can use the OSM slippy-map tiles as a raster source, but there are two small pieces that are easy to forget:

| What you have | What’s missing (or easy to overlook) |
|---------------|---------------------------------------|
| `type: 'raster'`<br>`tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png']` | **`tileSize: 256`** – OSM serves 256 px tiles, while MapLibre’s raster default is 512 px. Without it the imagery appears one zoom level off (blurred / shifted). |
|               | **`attribution`** (optional but required by OSM’s licence) – add a short string so the style complies with the tile provider’s terms. |
|               | **A raster layer that actually draws the source** – `addSource` alone does nothing; you need a `raster` layer that points at the source. |
|               | **Layer order** – if you add the layer after the basemap’s symbol layers it will cover street names. Insert it *below* the first `symbol` layer (or use `addLayer(..., firstSymbolId)`). |

### Minimal working example

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,               // ← required for correct zoom
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors',
  // optional: minzoom / maxzoom if you want to limit the range
  // minzoom: 0,
  // maxzoom: 19
});

// 2️⃣ Find the first symbol layer in the current style (so we can insert below it)
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;

// 3️⃣ Add a raster layer that uses the source
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'osm-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-basemap',
    paint: {
      // optional opacity, contrast, etc.
      // 'raster-opacity': 1.0
    }
  },
  firstSymbolId   // ← insert below labels; omit to append on top
);
```

### Why each piece matters

* **`tileSize: 256`** – Without it MapLibre assumes 512 px tiles, so a tile that is actually 256 px is interpreted as “half-zoomed”. The map looks blurry and the raster appears at the wrong scale, but no error is thrown.
* **`attribution`** – OSM’s licence requires you to display attribution somewhere on the page. The style spec lets you embed it in the source; you can also render it yourself in the UI.
* **Layer insertion** – Adding the raster layer after the style’s label (`symbol`) layers will hide those labels. Passing the ID of the first symbol layer as the second argument to `addLayer` guarantees the basemap stays underneath the text and icons.
* **CORS** – `tile.openstreetmap.org` already sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so cross-origin requests work out-of-the-box.

### Quick checklist before you run

1. **Source definition** includes `tileSize: 256` (and optional `minzoom`/`maxzoom`/`attribution`).  
2. **A raster layer** that references the source (`source: 'osm-basemap'`).  
3. **Insert the layer** before the first `symbol` layer (or at the very bottom of the style) if you want to keep existing labels visible.  
4. Verify network tab: you should see successful `200` PNG requests for tiles like `https://tile.openstreetmap.org/0/0/0.png`.  

Once those steps are in place the OSM tiles will render correctly in your MapLibre map. Happy mapping!

**What’s missing?**  
Your GeoJSON features have an identifier (`parcel_id`) but the source never tells MapLibre to use that property as the feature **id**. Without an id, `map.setFeatureState()` has nothing to attach the state to, so the call succeeds silently, the `feature-state` expression never matches, and `map.getFeatureState()` returns an empty object.

The fix is to add **`promoteId`** to the GeoJSON source (or otherwise supply an id) and then use that id when you call `setFeatureState`.  

Below is a complete, minimal wiring that makes hover highlighting work.

---

## 1. Define the GeoJSON source with `promoteId`

```js
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/parcels.geojson',   // or a raw GeoJSON object
  promoteId: 'parcel_id'                         // <-- tell MapLibre to use this property as the feature id
});
```

* `promoteId` can be a string (applies to every source-layer) or an object `{<sourceLayer>: <property>}` for vector sources.  
* For a plain GeoJSON source you only need the string form.

---

## 2. Add a layer that uses `feature-state` for the hover style

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'parcels-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',
  paint: {
    // default fill colour
    'fill-color': '#88c0d0',
    // colour when the feature’s hover state is true
    'fill-color': [
      'case',
      ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
      '#ffcc00',          // hover colour
      '#88c0d0'           // normal colour
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.6
  }
});
```

* The `feature-state` expression looks for a boolean key called `hover`.  
* Because the source now has real ids, the expression can resolve the state.

---

## 3. Wire the mouse events

```js
let hoveredId = null;

map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', (e) => {
  if (!e.features.length) return;

  const feature = e.features[0];
  const id = feature.id;               // <-- this is the promoted parcel_id

  // If we moved to a new feature, clear the old hover state
  if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
    map.setFeatureState(
      { source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId },
      { hover: false }
    );
  }

  // Set hover state on the new feature
  map.setFeatureState(
    { source: 'parcels', id: id },
    { hover: true }
  );

  hoveredId = id;
});

map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState(
      { source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId },
      { hover: false }
    );
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});
```

* `feature.id` is automatically populated from `parcel_id` because of `promoteId`.  
* The `setFeatureState` calls now receive a valid id, so the state is stored and the paint expression reacts.

---

## 4. Verify that the id is being promoted (optional debug)

```js
// After the source has loaded
map.on('data', (e) => {
  if (e.sourceId === 'parcels' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
    const first = map.getSource('parcels')._data.features[0];
    console.log('First feature id (promoted):', first.id); // should be the parcel_id value
  }
});
```

If `first.id` prints the expected `parcel_id`, the promotion worked.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls that cause the same symptom

| Symptom | Typical cause | Fix |
|---------|---------------|-----|
| `setFeatureState` does nothing, no console error | No feature id (missing `promoteId` or `generateId:true` on a source that already has a business key) | Add `promoteId` pointing to the property that uniquely identifies the feature |
| Hover works on the first feature only, then stops | Using `generateId:true` – ids are assigned by array index and change when the source data is refreshed | Use `promoteId` instead; keep ids stable across updates |
| State never read back (`getFeatureState` returns `{}`) | Wrong source name or id supplied to `setFeatureState` | Ensure the `source` field matches the source id (`'parcels'`) and `id` matches the promoted id |
| Paint expression never changes | `source-layer` supplied for a GeoJSON source (not needed) or expression typo | Remove `source-layer` for GeoJSON; use the exact expression shown above |

---

## 6. Full minimal example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Parcel hover with feature-state</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.45, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ source with promoteId
    map.addSource('parcels', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://example.com/parcels.geojson',
      promoteId: 'parcel_id'
    });

    // 2️⃣ layer that reacts to feature-state
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'parcels-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'parcels',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': [
          'case',
          ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
          '#ffcc00',
          '#88c0d0'
        ],
        'fill-opacity': 0.6
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ hover handling
    let hoveredId = null;
    map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', (e) => {
      if (!e.features.length) return;
      const id = e.features[0].id;
      if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
      }
      map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: id }, { hover: true });
      hoveredId = id;
    });
    map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
      if (hoveredId !== null) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
        hoveredId = null;
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run this (replace the GeoJSON URL with your own) and you’ll see the parcel under the mouse turn the hover colour. The key piece that makes it work is the **`promoteId: 'parcel_id'`** on the source. Once that’s present, `setFeatureState` and the `feature-state` expression behave as expected.
